14:50:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not uniquely British—most US detached houses, for example, have timber joists and timber/OSB subfloors upstairs, usually with carpet, hardwood, or other finishes on top. Concrete upper floors are common in US apartments, condos, basements, and some regions, but are not the normal construction for a typical American wood-frame house. Your experience does reflect a real regional difference, though: - **France and Spain:** Reinforced-concrete frames and concrete slab floors are very common, especially in urban housing. They suit masonry/infill walls, provide good fire and acoustic performance, and are part of the established construction industry and building tradition. Spain also has strong reasons to use reinforced concrete because of its climate, durability expectations, and, in some areas, seismic design. - **UK:** Traditional housing commonly combines brick/block external walls with **timber upper floors and roof structures**. This became an entrenched, economical system for low-rise homes. The building trade, standard designs, supply chain, regulations, and repair practices all support it. - **US:** Timber-frame construction is even more widespread than in the UK for houses. A typical upper floor is dimensional lumber or engineered timber joists, with plywood/OSB decking. Concrete is not automatically “better”; it solves different problems. It gives a solid feel, better airborne/impact sound isolation when properly detailed, and good fire resistance, but it is heavy, slower, and needs more substantial supporting structure. For a two-storey UK house, a timber floor meets the required structural, fire, and acoustic standards at lower cost and with easier routing of pipes and wiring. Also, a floor that feels solid or is tiled is not necessarily a structural concrete slab: it may be screed, tile backer, or a thick finish over a timber floor. But in much of continental Europe, your observation that upper floors are genuinely concrete is broadly right.
