14:57:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Britain’s damp climate was an important influence, but not because timber was uniquely necessary there. The key issue was usually **ground floors**, not upper floors. Traditional UK houses often used suspended timber ground floors: joists were raised above the soil with ventilated voids and air bricks, helping keep moisture away from the timber and interior. That made sense where damp ground and limited damp-proofing technology were concerns. For **upper floors**, timber joists were mainly used because they were relatively light, affordable, readily available, and easy to span between masonry walls. They did not need ventilation in the same way as a ground floor. Similar damp, rainy climates elsewhere—especially Ireland, northern Europe, North America, coastal Canada, New Zealand, and parts of Japan—also developed extensive timber construction. Meanwhile, concrete can work perfectly well in wet climates when detailed with appropriate damp-proofing and drainage. So climate helped shape British building practice, particularly suspended ground floors, but it does not explain timber upper floors as a uniquely British invention or system.
